 case  should be investigated by somebody other  than the Winnipeg Police Service and  then re-examined by new prosecutors in  question period yesterday Deputy Premier  Cliff Cullen said Manitoba is not  getting involved as the wheels of  Justice are turning elsewhere but that  is in fact not true it is over a year  since all eight of the Winnipeg  survivors who had their cases brought  forward by Winnipeg Police there were  more than 20 who gave statements they  were all denied charges over a year ago  there is no more Justice process  happening for anyone any victim or  Survivor of Peter Nygaard in Winnipeg or  anywhere in Manitoba nyard is facing  charges of sexually abusing and  trafficking dozens of women and girls in  Toronto New York and the Bahamas he's  been denied bail and remains in custody  pending an extradition hearing to the  U.S Melissa Ridge in Global News  Winnipeg  there are countless stories of people  risking everything to flee their country  and this one image from Spain's Coast  Guard says far more about desperation  than any words can convey these three  men from Nigeria were spotted on the  rudder of an oil tanker they survived 11  days at Sea as the ship sailed from  Nigeria to the Canary Islands all three  needed to be treated for hypothermia and  dehydration according to the Red Cross  more than twenty thousand refugees  attempted to cross from West Africa to  Europe last year and it's believed more  than one thousand died along the Route  veterans helping veterans next how-to  ex-military members are supporting  comrades off the battlefield  many veterans have encountered and  endured Unthinkable events while  deployed and because of that they come  home with psychological scars that  simmer beneath the surface for years now  a new program has been designed for  police officers to help military  veterans in crisis it was created by  Veterans for veterans and as our Ottawa  bureau chief Mercedes Stevenson reports  it's giving those who served and  sacrificed the help they deserve  on the battlefield The Perils of War are  often in plain sight but the trauma of  those experiences can linger long after  troops make it home we lost 14 on our  tour  but I lost I lost several more when I  came home  and uh I don't I don't know what's more  tragic after deploying to Afghanistan  courses in 2009 Jeremy Burns says he  came back to Canada a different person  struggling to fit into the society he  fought to defend being hyper Vigilant in  a war zone is fantastic but if I'm  switched on and Hyper Vigilant all the  time at home that's exhausting and  that's that really sets you apart from  everybody else Burns found kinship with  fellow veteran Aaron Dale meeting in  2018 when both signed up to join the  Toronto Police Service Dale served in  the Canadian Special Operations regiment  an elite unit that deploys around the  world it's highly skilled and tight-knit  leaving proved harder than expected had  a lot of confusion and a lot of  uncertainty Dale credits burns with  helping him cope with the loss of  identity he felt and encouraging him to  seek help there's a lot of help out  there but it's difficult to navigate and  it's difficult to ask for help and it's  difficult to do that when you're  suffering as a person the two veterans  realized their struggle was not unique  as the they encountered other veterans  in crisis while on the job men and women  who had served now on the other end of a  9-1-1 call  one of those veterans was Garrett Oliver  who was homeless and living in his truck  when he first met Dale in the middle of  a mental health crisis having a police  officer who was a veteran who knew which  resources could help beyond the  immediate crisis was invaluable in  turning Oliver's life around he was able  to guide me to the down a path that I  needed to go to in order to fix my  circumstance and my life  um  I owe him and Jeremy the world  for what they did for me  experiences like that led the two police  officers to create the military veterans  wellness program for police officers to  help veterans in crisis launched earlier  this summer the program is now available  to Police Services across the country  it aims to train officers on how to  interact with veterans mindful of both  their military training and the  potential experience of severe trauma  Burns and Dale enlisted an expert  psychologist and forensic psychiatrist  to develop the program combining  clinical expertise with their first-hand  experience as veterans the program also  provides a simple referral form officers  can use to connect veterans with the  specialized services and programs that  they're entitled to receive services and  programs that can be difficult to access  or even know about no matter what  struggle it is that they're struggling  with there is something available to  help them we just have to get to a point  where we're all comfortable asking for  that help Byrne says he hopes the  program will help those who have served  their country so selflessly to now get  the help they need and deserve Mercedes  Stevenson Global News  Ottawa  and that is global national for this  Tuesday I'm Donna Friesen tonight's your  Canada is the Japanese garden at Hatley  Park in call Wood British Columbia we'd  love to see your Canada please email it  to us and thanks for watching hope to  see you here again tomorrow bye-bye  thank you  foreign  [Music]
 

